  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening agricultural markets and food security (SAFE) - Mercy Corps/ADRA - Mercy Corps/ADRA Sudan</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">St&#xe4;rkande av jordbruksmarknaderna och livsmedelstryggheten - Mercy Corps/ADRA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">De internationella organisationen Mercy Corps genomf&#xf6;r tillsammans med Adventist Development and Relief Agency (ADRA) ett projekt f&#xf6;r st&#xe4;rkande av jordbruksmarknader och livsmedelstrygghet, &quot;Strengthening Agricultural Markets and Food Security (SAFE)&quot;, i Sudan under perioden 2019-2026. 

Insatsen syftar till att &#xf6;ka jordbruksproduktionen genom milj&#xf6;m&#xe4;ssigt h&#xe5;llbart jordbruk och b&#xe4;ttre fungerande jordbruksmarknader. Insatsen fokuserar p&#xe5; tre respektive fyra omr&#xe5;den i delstaterna Bl&#xe5; Nilen och S&#xf6;dra Kordofan.

Insatsen bygger till stor del p&#xe5; en marknadssystemansats (MSD) med ett adaptivt arbetss&#xe4;tt vilket inneb&#xe4;r att projektets aktiviteter kan komma att f&#xf6;r&#xe4;ndras under aktivitetsperioden. En MSD-ansats syftar till att adressera grundorsaker till att marknader som &#xe4;r av vikt f&#xf6;r m&#xe4;nniskor som lever i fattigdom inte tillr&#xe4;ckligt gagnar dessa grupper, vilket p&#xe5; sikt ska resultera i l&#xe5;ngsiktiga f&#xf6;r&#xe4;ndringar som kan skalas upp till att n&#xe5; m&#xe5;nga. Detta kan ocks&#xe5; inneb&#xe4;ra att det tar l&#xe4;ngre tid att se konkreta resultat men att dessa &#xe4;r uth&#xe5;lliga n&#xe4;r de v&#xe4;l uppn&#xe5;s. Ansatsen kommer d&#xe4;rf&#xf6;r inneb&#xe4;ra att Sida beh&#xf6;ver ta st&#xe4;llning till f&#xf6;r&#xe4;ndringar i programmet och vara en aktiv dialogpart.

Det &#xf6;vergripande m&#xe5;let med insatsen &#xe4;r f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttrad livsmedelsf&#xf6;rs&#xf6;rjning f&#xf6;r 20 000 sm&#xe5;b&#xf6;nder och deras familjer, motsvarande 120 000 personer,  genom &#xf6;kat och j&#xe4;mst&#xe4;llt marknadstilltr&#xe4;de och &#xf6;kad jordbruksproduktion. M&#xe5;let f&#xf6;rv&#xe4;ntas uppn&#xe5;s genom att uppfylla f&#xf6;ljande f&#xf6;rv&#xe4;ntade resultat:

1. Manliga och kvinnliga sm&#xe5;b&#xf6;nder har &#xf6;kad och mer klimatresilient produktion
2. Manliga och kvinnliga sm&#xe5;b&#xf6;nder &#xf6;kar till&#xe4;mpningen av h&#xe5;llbar naturresursanv&#xe4;ndning
3. Manliga och kvinnliga sm&#xe5;b&#xf6;nder har f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttrade marknadskopplingar (tillg&#xe5;ng till finansiering och insatsvaror f&#xf6;r jordbruket)
4. &#xd6;kad j&#xe4;mst&#xe4;lldhet mellan m&#xe4;n, kvinnor, pojkar och flickor och mer egenmakt till kvinnor och flickor verksamma inom marknader av vikt f&#xf6;r det sm&#xe5;skaliga jordbruket.

Det svenska bidraget till projektet &#xe4;r MSEK 199.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The international organisation Mercy Corps, in collaboration with the Adventist Development and Relief Agency (ADRA), is implementing the project &quot;Strengthening Agricultural Markets and Food Security (SAFE)&quot; in Sudan for during the period 2019-2026. The intervention will be carried out by a consortia of Mercy Corps and ADRA, where the former is the agreement partner to Sida. 

The intervention aims at improving agricultural production through environmentally sustainable farming and improved marketing systems. The geographical areas chosen are Blue Nile (BNS) and South Kordofan (SKS) states in Sudan targeting 3 localities and 4 localities in BNS and SKS respectively. 

The intervention is to a large extent based on a Market Systems Approach (MSD) applying adaptive management, which means that changes of project activities might take place during the project period. A MSD approach aims to address underlying causes of poor performance in specific markets that matter to people living in poverty, resulting in scalable long-term changes. This may also mean that it takes longer to see concrete results, but that these should be sustainable once they are achieved. The approach will therefore mean that Sida needs to accept changes in the programme and be an active dialogue partner.

The common rationale is to address the underlying causes of poor performance in specific markets that matter to poor people, producing scalable long-term change.

The overall goal of the programme is improved food security for 20,000 male and female smallholder farmer households through enhanced gender equitable market access and agricultural production  To achieve the project goal, the project has the following objectives:

1. Male and female smallholder farmers have increased and more climate resilient yields
2. Male and female smallholder farmers increase sustainable natural resources management practices
3. Male and female smallholder farmers have improved market linkages (access to finance and agricultural inputs) 
4. Increased equality between men, women, boys and girls and increased empowerment of women and girls in smallholder agricultural market systems.

The Swedish contribution to the project is MSEK 199.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The main goal of the Strengthening Agriculture Market and Food Security SAFE project is to improve the food security for 31 000 male and female smallholder farmer households across South Kordofan and Blue Nile states, through enhanced gender equitable market access and agricultural production resilience. The project is guided by a Market Systems Development (MSD) and resilience approaches, to sustain food security gains despite the complex risk environment in which smallholder farmers are exposed to. The project is centred around five key objectives:

Objective 1: Increased yields for vulnerable male and female smallholder farmers.

Objective 2: Increased sustainable natural resource management practices.

Objective 3: Male and female smallholder farmers have improved market linkages (access to finance and agricultural inputs).

Objective 4: Increased gender equity between men, women, boys and girls and increased empowerment of women and girls in smallholder agricultural market systems.

Objective 5: Increased preparedness and ability to respond to disasters.

In August 2023 the final Evaluation Data Analysis of the SAFE programme was produced (scheduled at the end of the original implementation period). The study was solid and lessons/recommendations from this work are referred to in parts below and in connection with the results from implementation year four (4) 2022-2023.</narrative>
  </description>
